/**
 
@page page_multigrid_numerics_Mixed_Galerkin Mixed Galerkin formulations
 
A brief, informal discussion of mixed Galerkin methods.

<!-- Add this one for href links to subitems -->
\tableofcontents

This is a brief discussion of key ideas behind Mixed Galerkin (DG) methods. 
While we discuss some of the mathematics behind mixed formulations, the focus
of this text is informal (from a maths point of view) but puts special emphasis
on how numerical concepts are mapped onto Peano's data structures and mesh 
traversal. 
We focus notably on the combination of mixed methods with 
@ref page_multigrid_numerics_Disontinuous_Galerkin "Discontinous Galerkin" methods.
One can use mixed continuous Galerkin methods, but they are not particular 
widespread due to multiple numerical issues.


# Method

Assume we aim to solve

@f$ - \nabla ^2 u = - \partial_x^2 u - \partial_y^2 u = f, @f$

which is a second-order equation. Let us first introduce a helper vector @f$ \phi = (\phi _x, \phi _y) @f$, so we can instead 
write in a first-order formulation:

@f$ \phi_x = \partial_x u, \ \phi_y = \partial_y u @f$. 

We note that @f$ \phi @f$ is a @f$ d @f$-dimensional vector, which contains the
gradient of @f$ u @f$ in each dimension. Some textbooks prefer to use 
@f$ (u,v) @f$, but we avoid this to be dimension generic: the @f$ \phi @f$ is
just a factor. We also avoid to use @f$ p @f$, another popular choice for 
unknowns anywhere in this text, as the @f$ p @f$ canonically is used for 
pressure in CFD applications.

The whole introduction of the helper variable allows us to rewrite the PDE in 
first order, noting that the left hand side is just a divergence:

\f{eqnarray*}{
- \partial_x \phi_x - \partial_y \phi_y = - \text{div} \ \phi & = & f, \\
  \phi_x - \partial_x u & = & 0, \\
  \phi_y - \partial_y u & = & 0. 
\f}


We can write the second and third line as one with the gradient:
\f{eqnarray*}{
  - \text{div} \ \phi & = & f, \\
  \phi - \nabla u & = & 0. 
\f}

Nothing has been gained so far, despite the fact that the gradient is now 
explicitly available in our formulation. Some codes can use this for their 
algorithm. We have indeed "lost" a little bit of efficiency. In the normal
@ref tutorials_multigrid_petsc_continuous_galerkin_poisson "continuous Galerkin case", for example, 
we have only one unknown, i.e. a scalar, per degree of freedom. This time, we have 
have to maintain three unknowns.


This means, we might have had 

           unknowns_per_cell_dof = 1

in a continuous Galerkin setup for a scalar equation, but now we need to use

           unknowns_per_cell_dof = 1+Dimensions


## The weak formulation

If we want to solve the arising system of equations (with multiple equations due
to the mixed approach), we have to 
test both the first, second, third, ... line. 
We draw test functions from the solution space (the original PDE) and 
from the helper spaces. Let us call them @f$ v_u @f$ and @f$ v_\phi @f$.

Assume we start from a scalar PDE such as the Poisson equation.
In this example, one tests against u and one tests against the gradient space. 
Therefore, @f$ \psi _u: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R}^2 @f$ and @f$ \psi _\phi: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R} @f$, as the latter tests the scalar equation.


Let us call them @f$ \psi _u @f$ and @f$ \psi _\phi @f$.
One tests against u and one tests against the gradient space. 
Therefore, @f$ \psi _u: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R}^2 @f$ and @f$ \psi _\phi: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R} @f$, as the latter tests the scalar equation.


As we take all these tests from the same function space as the shape functions,
we know that they all have local support. Integration hence yields

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int _\Omega ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi _\phi ) dx &=& \int_{\text{cell}_\phi} ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi _\phi ) dx  \ \ \ \forall \psi _\phi 
  \\  
   &=& \int_{\text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} ( \phi \nabla \psi _\phi ) dx - \int_{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} (n \cdot \phi) \psi _\phi dS(x)  
   = \int_{\text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} f \cdot \psi _\phi dx,
\f}

for the first equation where the @f$ n @f$ is the outer normal of the cell. The
second equation gives us 

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int _\Omega ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx - \int _\Omega ( \nabla u, \psi _u ) dx
   &=& \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx - \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \nabla u, \psi _u ) dx
  \\  
   &=& \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx + \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot \text{div} \psi _u dx
    - \int _{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot (n, \psi _u) dS(x) = 0.
\f}

In the first line, we exploit the fact that the support of the test function is 
localised. Even though the integral originally spanned the whole domain, it 
boils down to an integral over one cell only.
After that, we use standard integration by parts. 
At this point, we only note that some terms here do not exist:

- @f$ (n, \phi ) @f$ does not exist on the faces of a cell. It jumps. The term  @f$ \int_{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} (n \cdot \phi) \psi _\phi dS(x) @f$ in the first weak formulation therefore does not make sense.
- @f$ u @f$ does not exist on the faces of a cell. It jumps. The term  @f$ \int _{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot (n, \psi _u) dS(x) @f$ in the second weak formulation therefore does not make sense. 


We obtain a system 

@f$ A x = b @f$

where x has @f$ K |\mathbb{C}| \cdot (p+1)^d @f$ entries, where @f$ \mathbb{C}  @f$ 
is set of cells in the mesh.



# Example: the Poisson equation

Let the linear operator @f$ \mathcal{L}(u) @f$ denote the Laplace operator. 
The Poisson equation then yields 


\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int _\Omega ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi _\phi ) dx &=& \int_{\text{cell}_\phi} ( -\text{div} \ \phi, \psi _\phi ) dx  \ \ \ \forall \psi _\phi 
  \\  
   &=& \int_{\text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} ( \phi \nabla \psi _\phi ) dx - \int_{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} (n \cdot \phi) \psi _\phi dS(x)  
   = \int_{\text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} f \cdot \psi _\phi dx,
\f}

for the first equation where the @f$ n @f$ is the outer normal of the cell. The
second equation gives us 

\f{eqnarray*}{
  \int _\Omega ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx - \int _\Omega ( \nabla u, \psi _u ) dx
   &=& \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx - \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \nabla u, \psi _u ) dx
  \\  
   &=& \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}} ( \phi, \psi _u ) dx + \int _{\text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot \text{div} \psi _u dx
    - \int _{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot (n, \psi _u) dS(x) = 0.
\f}

In the first line, we exploit the fact that the support of the test function is 
localised. Even though the integral originally spanned the whole domain, it 
boils down to an integral over one cell only.
After that, we use standard integration by parts. 
At this point, we only note that some terms here do not exist:

- @f$ (n, \phi ) @f$ does not exist on the faces of a cell. It jumps. The term  @f$ \int_{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _\phi }} (n \cdot \phi) \psi _\phi dS(x) @f$ in the first weak formulation therefore does not make sense.
- @f$ u @f$ does not exist on the faces of a cell. It jumps. The term  @f$ \int _{\partial \text{cell}_{\psi _u}}  u \cdot (n, \psi _u) dS(x) @f$ in the second weak formulation therefore does not make sense. 



